POW Systems =E2=80=94 Sensor/Actuator + Repair Economics Data Garden Core b=
oundary:
POW vs Muse

This should be explicit in the source-of-truth spec:

*POW models what work is economically available in the world. Muse
determines what work is available to you.*

A strong implementation rule:

*POW must be capable of producing its answer without knowing who the user
is.*

POW owns:

   - Which device models/components are failing.
   - Which replacement parts, sensors and actuators are involved.
   - Component price, stock, lead time, lifecycle and substitutions.
   - Working-vs-broken resale spread.
   - Repair difficulty, disassembly steps and documentation coverage.
   - Required capabilities, skills, qualifications and occupations.
   - Training supply, apprenticeship flow and occupational supply.
   - Safety/recall information.
   - Geographic market state, repair prices, demand and supply.
   - Objective repair-economics metrics.

Muse owns:

   - =E2=80=9CCan I repair this?=E2=80=9D
   - =E2=80=9CAm I qualified?=E2=80=9D
   - =E2=80=9CShould I buy this broken washer?=E2=80=9D
   - =E2=80=9CWhich job should I take this afternoon?=E2=80=9D
   - Personalized recommendations using the user=E2=80=99s tools, qualifica=
tions,
   budget, location, travel radius, schedule and preferences.
   - Executing/bookings/workflow around the individual.

Example:
POW can say: =E2=80=9COldham washing-machine repair spread =3D =C2=A3X; com=
mon failure
component =3D Y; part stock =3D Z.=E2=80=9D
Muse can say: =E2=80=9CGiven Gary=E2=80=99s skills/tools/location/calendar,=
 he should
inspect this listing/job.=E2=80=9D
Why sensors + actuators are a major POW primitive

Most automated physical systems reduce to:

REAL WORLD
=E2=86=92 SENSORS
=E2=86=92 CONTROL / COMPUTATION
=E2=86=92 ACTUATORS
=E2=86=92 REAL WORLD

If intelligence/computation becomes dramatically cheaper, the physical
boundary becomes relatively more valuable:

   - temperature / pressure / position / flow / current / vibration /
   vision / force sensing
   - motors / pumps / valves / relays / solenoids / servos / compressors /
   fans / locks / heaters

These components have excellent POW economics:

   - physical wear
   - exact compatibility
   - finite inventory
   - failure events
   - replacement markets
   - repair labour
   - secondary-market spreads
   - supply chains
   - skills/training requirements

This fits the Data Garden thesis exactly:

*unique transformation =C3=97 continuous collection =C3=97 time*

The moat is not the raw catalogue. It is the continuously transformed
historical economic state.
Best initial data sources P0 =E2=80=94 Open Repair Alliance

Open Repair Alliance publishes hundreds of thousands of row-level
electrical/electronic repair attempts under CC BY-SA.

Useful fields include:

   - product category
   - brand
   - product age
   - fault/problem
   - repair outcome
   - repairability outcome
   - repair barriers, including unavailable/expensive parts and missing
   repair information

This is almost perfect training data for a POW failure/repairability layer.

Important opportunity:
ORA intentionally dropped model-level identity from the standard because
identifiers were inconsistent/noisy.

That means POW can own a missing transformation:

messy repair description
=E2=86=92 brand/product family
=E2=86=92 model evidence
=E2=86=92 fault/component inference
=E2=86=92 canonical device/model
=E2=86=92 OEM component / replacement MPN / substitutes

Identity resolution becomes part of the moat.

Source:
https://openrepair.org/open-data/downloads/
P0+++ =E2=80=94 France repairability/durability open data

France=E2=80=99s official repairability/durability datasets are exceptional=
ly
aligned with POW.

The washing-machine durability schema can include:

   - exact model identity
   - repairability score
   - reliability score
   - professional repair-documentation URL
   - consumer repair-documentation URL
   - named spare-part classes
   - disassembly-step counts
   - part delivery delays
   - years of spare-part availability
   - availability separated for professional repairers, producers,
   distributors and consumers

Example part classes include:

   - pumps
   - thermostats / sensors
   - pressure switches
   - motors / brushes
   - door-lock assemblies
   - control/power boards
   - heating elements
   - hoses / Aquastop
   - displays
   - belts
   - bearings / drums
   - firmware

This gives us a prebuilt repair ontology:

DEVICE
=E2=94=9C COMPONENT
=E2=94=9C COMPONENT TYPE
=E2=94=9C SENSOR/ACTUATOR?
=E2=94=9C REPAIR ACCESS
=E2=94=9C DISASSEMBLY COMPLEXITY
=E2=94=9C DOCUMENTATION
=E2=94=9C PART AVAILABILITY
=E2=94=9C DELIVERY LATENCY
=E2=94=94 SUPPORT HORIZON

Use it immediately as an ontology/training source even if France is not the
target market.

Sources:
https://schema.data.gouv.fr/etalab/schema-indice-reparabilite/0.1.1/
https://github.com/etalab/schema-indice-durabilite
P0 =E2=80=94 EPREL

The EU EPREL registry provides a massive product-identity/specification
surface across energy-labelled products.

Useful for:

   - model identity
   - GTIN
   - technical specs
   - manuals / repair information
   - energy/product parameters
   - spare-parts/support metadata where available

Canonical identity target:

BRAND

   - OEM MODEL
   - GTIN
   - EPREL ID
   - MPN
   - marketplace aliases
   =E2=86=92 POW PRODUCT ID

Identity resolution needs to precede sophisticated modelling.

Source:
https://energy-efficient-products.ec.europa.eu/eprel_en
Distributor/parts market

POW should continuously snapshot individual components.
Mouser =E2=80=94 P0

Useful API fields:

   - MPN
   - manufacturer
   - price tiers
   - availability
