Canonical product thesis
<p>POW should become a temporal graph answering:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>What physical things are failing, what fixes them, what those fi=
xes cost, who can perform them, how scarce those people and parts are, and =
how every one of those quantities is changing?</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then the strict division remains:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>POW models what work is economically available in the world. Mus=
e determines what work is available to you.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is one of the strongest extensions of POW Systems because the same =
infrastructure serves:</p>
<ul>
<li>repair-market intelligence</li>
<li>physical-component scarcity</li>
<li>resale/flipping economics</li>
<li>supplier/distributor monitoring</li>
<li>dropshipping/commerce opportunity discovery</li>
<li>labour/training bottlenecks</li>
<li>robot/edge-AI Seesaw modelling</li>
<li>long-horizon proprietary historical data</li>
</ul>
<p>The data garden starts useful immediately and gets structurally harder t=
o recreate with every day of collection.</p>

